<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">4 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">7 events that happened on 4 March, from 1152 to 1966.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="76" height="76"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1152 <span class="tih-ago">874 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Frederick Barbarossa elected King of Germany</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The German princes chose Frederick of Hohenstaufen as king at Frankfurt. Crowned emperor in 1155, he fought repeated campaigns in Italy and clashed with the papacy until his death on crusade in 1190.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/england" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/england.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="76" height="76"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1461 <span class="tih-ago">565 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Edward IV proclaimed King of England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Yorkist Edward, Earl of March, was proclaimed king in London, deposing the Lancastrian Henry VI. Weeks later he won the bloody Battle of Towton, securing his throne.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1789 <span class="tih-ago">237 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">US Constitution takes effect as the first Congress meets</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The new federal government began operation as Congress gathered at Federal Hall in New York, though it took weeks to reach a quorum. The Constitution is the oldest written national constitution still in force.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1861 <span class="tih-ago">165 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Abraham Lincoln inaugurated as 16th President</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">With seven states already seceded, Lincoln appealed to 'the better angels of our nature' in his inaugural address. War began at Fort Sumter five weeks later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="76" height="76"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1877 <span class="tih-ago">149 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Tchaikovsky's 'Swan Lake' premieres at the Bolshoi</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The first production in Moscow was poorly received. Revived in St Petersburg in 1895 with choreography by Petipa and Ivanov, it became the most famous of all ballets.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1933 <span class="tih-ago">93 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Franklin D. Roosevelt inaugurated in the depths of the Depression</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">With banks failing nationwide, Roosevelt told Americans 'the only thing we have to fear is fear itself'. In his first hundred days Congress passed a wave of New Deal legislation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="76" height="76"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1966 <span class="tih-ago">60 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">John Lennon says the Beatles are 'more popular than Jesus'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The remark appeared in an interview in London's Evening Standard and passed unnoticed in Britain. Reprinted in the US months later, it led to record burnings and radio bans.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li></ol>
